      "id": "lem-laurent-non-archimedean",
      "tail": "```text\nVERDICT: CLEAN\nBOUNDARY:\n  - n = 0 handled explicitly in step 1.2; h = 0 handled in step 2.1 and step 1.3 by case split.\n  - k may be any integer (including negative) in clauses 2,4. The proofs use L1/L3 which apply for all integer indices; t^{-k}>0 for all k∈Z (step 1.1).\n  - Clause 4 forward assumes vanishing for j≤k; proof step 2.1 correctly infers v(h)>k (or h=0) and then |h|<t^{-k}. Converse only requires vanishing for j<k; step 1.3 shows that |h|<t^{-k} forces h(j)=0 for j<k (while allowing h(k)≠0 if |lc(h)|<1). The asymmetry is accurately reflected.\n  - Cofinality (clause 3): L5 supplies a natural k>v(ε); the same computation works for every integer k>v(ε) without change.\n  - No counterexample to non-Archimedeanness: t is a specific element with v(t)=-1, and for every n∈ℕ the valuation comparison gives t > n·1_K (step 1.2). Hence t cannot satisfy the Archimedean condition (step 2.3).\n  - All uses of L3 require the nonzero hypotheses, which are satisfied in each step.\n\nSOURCES:\n  Every cited dependency supplied in the dispatch was inspected against its mention in the target:\n  def-formal-laurent-series, lem-laurent-valuation, thm-laurent-ordered-field, def-archimedean-field, def-ordered-field, def-abs-value, thm-int-ordered-ring, lem-nat-embeds-int, def-int-order.\n  The target’s fact summaries [L1]–[L5] faithfully quote or directly follow from the quoted passages of these sources.\n\nCHECKED:\n  The title, the full public statement (clauses 1–4), every numbered proof step (1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 3.1), and the Remarks section were all read and verified against the supplied sources and the proof logic. No defect was found.\n```"
